On Sun, 2020-03-08 at 13:14 -0700, Andrea Gilardi wrote:
> I have one question regarding PIT values for zeroinflatedpoisson0
> data. I run the following code (which is taken from the examples) for
> the estimation of a zeroinflatedpoisson0 model and I ask for the
> estimation of the CPO and PIT values:

> data <- list(y = y, z = z)
> formula <- y ~ 1 + z
> result0 <- inla(
>   formula,
>   family = "zeroinflatedpoisson0",
>   data = data,
>   E = E,
>   control.compute = list(cpo = TRUE)
> )
>
> Then I checked and none of the observations presented a "failure"
> (i.e. sum(result0$cpo$failure) is equal to 0) and I created a plot
> for the PIT. This is the result:
>

> 1) Since I am dealing with discrete data, the formula for the PITs
> should be: P(Y_i < y_i^obs | y_-i) + 0.5 P(Y_i = y_i^obs | y_-i),
> right?

What is implemented is

	Prob(Y <= y_i | y_{-i})

if you want the 1/2-correction you can get that one from the cpo, like

plot(r$cpo$pit - 0.5 * r$cpo$cpo)

sorry, there was a small bug in the pit calculations for the type0
poisson/binom/nbinom, which is fixed in new testing-version 20.03.09,
so please upgrade to that one and try again. [thanks to pointing me to
it!]

Best
H

